5. Explain how a phishing attack could target a Zomato or Swiggy user, and write a step-by-step guide for a new user on how to recognize and avoid such attacks. Include at least 3 warning signs to look for in fake emails or websites.
=>Example Attack Scenario
An attacker could send a fake message claiming:
"Your food delivery account has been suspended. Verify your account immediately to continue using the service." The message could contain a link leading to a fraudulent website that visually resembles a food-delivery application.
The attacker may try to convince the victim to enter information such as a username, password, OTP, or payment information.
Step-by-Step Guide to Recognize and Avoid the Attack:
Step 1 – Check the sender
Look carefully at the email address, phone number, or account that sent the
message.
Step 2 – Check the link
Do not immediately click a link. Check whether the destination belongs to
the legitimate service.
Step 3 – Look for urgency
Messages saying "your account will be deleted in 10 minutes" or "verify
within 24 hours" are common social-engineering techniques.
Step 4 – Never share OTPs
A legitimate support representative should not ask you to disclose an OTP
received on your phone.
Step 5 – Open the official application
Instead of using a link received in an unexpected message, open the
official food-delivery application directly.
Step 6 – Report suspicious messages
Use the email provider or application reporting mechanism to report
suspected phishing.
Three Major Warning Signs:
1. Suspicious or misspelled website domain
2. Urgent threats or unrealistic offers
3. Requests for passwords, OTPs, card information, or other sensitive
information

